Our first function

Write a function that calculates the area of a circle. Your function should take a single argument: radius.







NoteSolution






area_circle &lt;- function(radius) {
  return(pi * (radius^2))
}

area_circle(12)

[1] 452.3893






Write a function that calculates the area of a rectangle. The function should take two arguments: length and width.







NoteSolution






area_rectangle &lt;- function(length, width) {
  return(length * width)
}





Test your rectangle function with valid input (e.g., 12.5 for length and 8.7 for width).







NoteSolution






area_rectangle(12.5, 8.7)

[1] 108.75






Update your function to include default arguments (e.g., length = 3 and width = 2). Test by calling the function with no arguments.







NoteSolution






area_rectangle &lt;- function(length = 3, width = 2) {
  return(length * width)
}

area_rectangle()

[1] 6






What’s the difference between these two function calls?


area_rectangle(length = 2.1, width = 5)
area_rectangle(2.1, 5)







NoteSolution





There is no difference in the result. The first call names the arguments; the second matches them by position (length first, then width). Naming arguments makes your code easier to read, and means the order no longer matters.




Test your function with invalid input (e.g., -2 for length and 4 for width).


area_rectangle(-2, 4)


Update your previous answer to check if length and width are positive numbers. If not, the function should return an error message.







NoteSolution






area_rectangle &lt;- function(length, width) {
  if (!is.numeric(length) || !is.numeric(width)) {
    stop("Length and width must be numeric.")
  } else if (length &lt;= 0 || width &lt;= 0) {
    stop("Length and width must be positive numbers.")
  } else {
    return(length * width)
  }
}





Test the function with different data types, like characters or logical values, and observe the output.


area_rectangle("2", 4)
area_rectangle(TRUE, 4)

Plotting function

Write a function that plots a scatterplot for two vectors.

Test your function with mtcars$wt and mtcars$mpg.
Hint: Use plot(x, y).








NoteSolution






plot_xy &lt;- function(x, y) {
  plot(x, y)
}

plot_xy(mtcars$wt, mtcars$mpg)












Update your function to check that the provided vectors:

are numeric;
are the same length.

If not, return an informative error message (Hint: Use stop("...")).







NoteSolution






plot_xy &lt;- function(x, y) {
  if (length(x) != length(y)) {
    stop("Vectors must have the same length")
  } else if (!is.numeric(x) || !is.numeric(y)) {
    stop("Vectors must both be numeric")
  }
  plot(x, y)
}


# Testing the function
plot_xy(1:10, letters)   # Error: Vectors must have the same length
plot_xy(1:26, letters)   # Error: Vectors must both be numeric
plot_xy(1:26, rnorm(26)) # Success: Plots scatterplot

Fizz buzz
Fizz buzz is a word game to teach division. Players take turns to count incrementally, replacing any number divisible by three with the word “fizz”, any number divisible by five with the word “buzz”, and any number divisible by both 3 and 5 with the word “fizzbuzz”.

Write a fizzbuzz() function, taking a single number as input. The logic of the function is as follows:

If the number is divisible by three, it returns “fizz”.
If it’s divisible by five, it returns “buzz”.
If it’s divisible by three and five, it returns “fizzbuzz”.
Otherwise, it returns the number.








TipHint





You can use the modulo operator %% to check for divisibility. For example:

1:10 %% 3 == 0

 [1] FALSE FALSE  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE FALSE











NoteSolution






fizzbuzz &lt;- function(x) {
  # Check the input is a single numeric value
  stopifnot(length(x) == 1)
  stopifnot(is.numeric(x))
  if (!(x %% 3) && !(x %% 5)) {
    return("fizzbuzz")
  } else if (!(x %% 3)) {
    return("fizz")
  } else if (!(x %% 5)) {
    return("buzz")
  } else {
    return(as.character(x))
  }
}





Test your function on a few individual values, such as 3, 5, 15 and 7.







NoteSolution






fizzbuzz(3)

[1] "fizz"

fizzbuzz(5)

[1] "buzz"

fizzbuzz(15)

[1] "fizzbuzz"

fizzbuzz(7)

[1] "7"





To run your function on every number from 1 to 30, you can use sapply(), which applies a function to each element of a vector in turn. We’ll cover this properly in Session 5.

sapply(1:30, fizzbuzz)

 [1] "1"        "2"        "fizz"     "4"        "buzz"     "fizz"    
 [7] "7"        "8"        "fizz"     "buzz"     "11"       "fizz"    
[13] "13"       "14"       "fizzbuzz" "16"       "17"       "fizz"    
[19] "19"       "buzz"     "fizz"     "22"       "23"       "fizz"    
[25] "buzz"     "26"       "fizz"     "28"       "29"       "fizzbuzz"








